"""Reference-as-solution oracle for ``aten::_cast_Byte`` (FlagGems ``_cast_Byte``).

Declared entrypoint: ``run``.

The frozen Gems pytest that owns this contract is the witness for the ABI, so
``run`` keeps exactly the representative schema it calls,

    aten::_cast_Byte(Tensor self, bool non_blocking=False) -> Tensor

and the observable behaviour those tests assert:

* an already-``torch.uint8`` input is returned unchanged (same object, same
  storage, same strides, no copy) - the identity/no-copy effect the Gems
  pytest asserts with ``actual is x``;
* every other input is converted with the native ATen operator that the same
  tests use as ``expected``, so values (truncation, wraparound, fractional,
  out-of-range and non-finite inputs), the output dtype ``torch.uint8``,
  device, shape, the ``Preserve`` stride rule for non-overlapping-and-dense
  inputs and the non-grad result all follow the device's native semantics
  rather than a re-implementation guess.

This module intentionally does not import ``flag_gems`` and does not call the
generated candidate: the oracle is the native operator, never the
implementation under test.  It is not a stand-in for a CPU/comparison path -
the conversion happens on the device of ``self``.
"""

import torch

_NATIVE_CAST_BYTE = torch.ops.aten._cast_Byte.default


def run(self, non_blocking=False):
    """Cast ``self`` to ``torch.uint8`` with native overlay semantics."""
    if self.dtype == torch.uint8:
        # Byte -> Byte is a no-copy identity in the frozen pytest contract.
        return self
    return _NATIVE_CAST_BYTE(self, non_blocking)
